Here's the full **testsmells.org 19-smell academic audit** of `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`.

> **Integration-test calibration applied.** The class is annotated `[TestCategory("Integration")]`, so Mystery Guest (in-memory SQLite), Eager Test (multi-step workflows), and contextually-obvious count assertions are **not penalized**.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High | 3 | 3 |
| Medium | 1 | 1 |
| Low | 0 | 0 |
| **Total** | **4** | **4 of 7** |

---

## Findings by Severity

### ❶ Conditional Test Logic — **High**

**Smell:** `Conditional Test Logic` (testsmells.org)
**Test:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 93)
**Problem:** `if/else` on line 101 means only one assertion branch ever executes; the other is dead code. You can never tell from test results whether the `PremiumUser` path works.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — one branch is always dead
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Fix:** Split into two focused tests with deterministic setup:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_StandardUser_ReturnsName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = (PremiumUser)_repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com")!;
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Hidden coverage gap — one code path is never verified.

---

### ❷ Sleepy Test — **High**

**Smell:** `Sleepy Test` (testsmells.org)
**Test:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 82)
**Problem:** `Thread.Sleep(3000)` blocks for 3 seconds unconditionally. This is non-deterministic (may be too short on slow CI, always too long on fast machines), and the method is declared `async` but never uses `await`.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — hard 3-second wall-clock sleep
Thread.Sleep(3000);
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Fix:** Poll with a timeout or use an event-based wait:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    // Poll with back-off, max 5 seconds
    var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow.AddSeconds(5);
    while (DateTime.UtcNow < deadline)
    {
        if (_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
            break;
        await Task.Delay(100);
    }

    Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky CI failures, 3 s of wasted wall-clock time per run, and the blocking `Thread.Sleep` in an `async` method can starve the thread pool.

---

### ❸ Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test) — **High**

**Smell:** `Unknown Test` / `Empty Test` (testsmells.org)
**Test:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113)
**Problem:** Inserts 100 users but asserts nothing. If `Insert` silently drops every row, this test still passes.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — exercises code but verifies nothing
for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
{
    _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
}
// no assertion
```

**Fix:** Assert on the observable outcome:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

> *Note:* The method name implies a "does not throw" intent, which is acknowledged — but a test that merely proves no exception was thrown gives false confidence. Asserting on state is strictly better.

**Risk if unfixed:** False pass — 100% coverage with 0% verification. Bugs in bulk-insert logic go undetected.

---

### ❹ Assertion Roulette — **Medium**

**Smell:** `Assertion Roulette` (testsmells.org)
**Test:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (line 28)
**Problem:** 4 assertions, none with a diagnostic message. When one fails in CI, the stack trace shows `Assert.AreEqual failed` but not *which property* was wrong without reading the code.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — which assertion failed?
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

**Fix:** Add message parameters:

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated Id should be positive");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower diagnosis of CI failures — developers must read code instead of reading the failure message.

